'''Eliud Kipchoge''' {{Post-nominals|list=[[Order of the Golden Heart (Kenya)|EGH]]}} (born 5 November 1984) is a Kenyan [[long-distance runner]] who competes in the [[marathon]] and formerly specialized in the [[5000 metres]]. Kipchoge is the [[Athletics at the 2016 Summer Olympics – Men's marathon|2016]] and [[Athletics at the 2020 Summer Olympics – Men's marathon|2020]] Olympic marathon champion, and was the [[marathon world record progression|world record]] holder in the marathon from 2018 to 2023,<ref name="George Ramsay">{{Cite web |author=George Ramsay |title=Eliud Kipchoge is the 'greatest of all time ... in any sport' |date=8 August 2021 |url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/08/08/sport/eliud-kipchoge-olympic-marathon-spt-intl/index.html |access-date=20 March 2022 |publisher=CNN |archive-date=20 April 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230420060021/https://www.cnn.com/2021/08/08/sport/eliud-kipchoge-olympic-marathon-spt-intl/index.html |url-status=live }}</ref> until that record was broken by [[Kelvin Kiptum]] at the [[2023 Chicago Marathon]]. He is widely considered to be one of the greatest marathon runners of all time.<ref>{{cite web |title=All time Top lists – Marathon Men – Senior {{!}} until 8 October 2023 {{!}} All |url=https://worldathletics.org/records/all-time-toplists/road-running/marathon/outdoor/men/senior?regionType=world&drop=regular&fiftyPercentRule=regular&page=1&bestResultsOnly=false&firstDay=1899-12-30&lastDay=2023-10-08 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231010173029/https://worldathletics.org/records/all-time-toplists/road-running/marathon/outdoor/men/senior?regionType=world&drop=regular&fiftyPercentRule=regular&page=1&bestResultsOnly=false&firstDay=1899-12-28&lastDay=2023-10-08 |archive-date=10 October 2023 |access-date=10 October 2023 |website=[[World Athletics]]}}</ref><ref name="George Ramsay"/>

Kipchoge claimed his first individual world championship title in 2003 by winning the junior race at the [[2003 IAAF World Cross Country Championships|World Cross Country Championships]] and setting a [[List of junior world records in athletics|world junior record]] for the 5000 m. At the age of eighteen, he became the senior 5000&nbsp;m world champion at the [[2003 World Championships in Athletics|2003 World Championships]] with a championship record, then followed by an Olympic bronze for Kenya in [[Athletics at the 2004 Summer Olympics|2004]] and a bronze at the [[2006 IAAF World Indoor Championships|2006 World Indoor Championships]]. A five-time [[5000 metres at the World Championships in Athletics|World Championship 5000&nbsp;m]] finalist, Kipchoge took silver medals at the [[2007 World Championships in Athletics|2007 World Championships]], [[Athletics at the 2008 Summer Olympics|2008 Beijing Olympics]], and [[Athletics at the 2010 Commonwealth Games|2010 Commonwealth Games]].

He switched to [[road running]] in 2012 and made the second-fastest [[half marathon]] debut ever, at 59:25. In his marathon debut, he won the 2013 [[Hamburg Marathon]] in a course record time. His first victory at a [[World Marathon Major]] came at the [[Chicago Marathon]] in 2014, and he went on to become series champion a record five times – for 2016, 2017, 2018, 2019 and 2022. He has won the [[London Marathon]] a record four times and also holds the record for most [[Berlin Marathon]] wins with five, his latest coming in September 2023. With 15 victories in his first 18 marathons from 2013 to 2023, Kipchoge's only losses were a second-place finish behind [[Wilson Kipsang Kiprotich]] at the [[2013 Berlin Marathon]], where Kipsang broke the world record, an eighth-place finish at the [[2020 London Marathon]] and a sixth place in his debut at the [[Boston Marathon]] in [[2023 Boston Marathon|2023]].<ref name="News">{{Cite web |url=https://www.leichtathletik.de
ews/detail/20237-stunden-eliud-kipchoge-bestaetigt-in-london-seine-vormachtstellung/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="BBC Sport">{{Cite news |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/athletics/24322574 |title=Berlin marathon: Wilson Kipsang sets new world record |date=29 September 2013 |work=BBC Sport |access-date=1 October 2018 |archive-date=1 October 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181001182250/https://www.bbc.com/sport/athletics/24322574 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="runningmagazine.ca">{{cite web |last1=Snider-McGrath |first1=Ben |title=Shura Kitata wins London Marathon in sprint finish, Kipchoge 8th |url=https://runningmagazine.ca/sections/runs-races/shura-kitata-wins-london-marathon-in-sprint-finish-kipchoge-8th/ |website=Canadian Running Magazine |date=4 October 2020 |access-date=5 October 2020 |archive-date=24 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201124032136/https://runningmagazine.ca/sections/runs-races/shura-kitata-wins-london-marathon-in-sprint-finish-kipchoge-8th/ |url-status=live }}</ref> He has since 2024, been defeated in his last three marathons. Kipchoge's last world record run broke by 30 seconds his own 2018 world record, which was in turn a 78-second improvement over the existing best, the greatest improvement in a marathon world record time since 1967.

On 12 October 2019, Kipchoge ran the marathon distance for the [[Ineos 1:59 Challenge]] in [[Vienna]], achieving a time of 1:59:40.2, becoming the first person in recorded history to do a [[sub-two-hour marathon]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/12/sports/eliud-kipchoge-marathon-record.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |title=Eliud Kipchoge Breaks Two-Hour Marathon Barrier |author=Andrew Keh |date=12 October 2019 |access-date=12 October 2019 |archive-date=12 October 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191012082618/https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/12/sports/eliud-kipchoge-marathon-record.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The run did not count as a new marathon record, as he ran with specialized shoes, standard competition rules for pacing and fluids were not followed, and it was not an open event.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.runnersworld.com
ews/a29430499/eliud-kipchoge-ineos-159-challenge-result/ |title=History Made: Kipchoge Runs Under 2 Hours at INEOS 1:59 Challenge |first1=Brian |last1=Dalek |first2=Christa |last2=Sgobba |date=12 October 2019 |website=Runner's World |access-date=12 October 2019 |archive-date=12 October 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191012091735/https://www.runnersworld.com
ews/a29430499/eliud-kipchoge-ineos-159-challenge-result/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/sports/2019/10/12/eliud-kipchoge-breaks-two-hour-marathon-record/ |title=Kenya's Eliud Kipchoge just became the first marathon runner to break the 2-hour barrier |newspaper=The Washington Post |last=Hawkins |first=Derek |date=12 October 2019 |access-date=12 October 2019 |archive-date=12 October 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191012124800/https://www.washingtonpost.com/sports/2019/10/12/eliud-kipchoge-breaks-two-hour-marathon-record/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/athletics/50460861 |title=Eliud Kipchoge: The man, the methods & controversies behind 'moon-landing moment' |work=BBC Sport |date=19 November 2019 |access-date=19 November 2019 |archive-date=19 November 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191119123248/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/athletics/50460861 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Kipchoge was appointed [[Order of the Golden Heart (Kenya)|Elder of the Order of the Golden Heart]] by former Kenyan President [[Uhuru Kenyatta]] on 20 October 2019 in recognition of his sub-two-hour marathon.<ref>{{cite web |title=Kipchoge honoured with the Elder of the Order of the Golden Heart of Kenya |url=https://www.standardmedia.co.ke/ureport/article/2001346182/kipchoge-honoured-with-the-elder-of-the-order-of-the-golden-heart-of-kenya |website=[[The Standard (Kenya)|The Standard]] |access-date=29 September 2022 |archive-date=29 September 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220929081519/https://www.standardmedia.co.ke/ureport/article/2001346182/kipchoge-honoured-with-the-elder-of-the-order-of-the-golden-heart-of-kenya |url-status=live }}</ref> He was also named the 2019 [[BBC Sports Personality World Sport Star of the Year|BBC World Sport Star of the Year]]. In 2023 he was awarded the [[Princess of Asturias Award]] in the category "Sports".<ref name="Princess of Asturias Award 2023">[https://www.fpa.es/en/princess-of-asturias-awards/laureates/2023-eliud-kipchoge.html?especifica=0&idCategoria=0&anio=2023&especifica=0 Princess of Asturias Award 2023]</ref>

==Personal life==
Kipchoge was born on 5 November 1984 in Kapsisiywa, [[Nandi County]], in Kenya. He graduated from the Kaptel Secondary School in Nandi County in 1999 but did not run seriously or as a profession then.<ref name="WAprofile">{{cite web |title=Eliud KIPCHOGE – Athlete Profile |url=https://worldathletics.org/athletes/kenya/eliud-kipchoge-14208194 |website=[[World Athletics]] |access-date=1 January 2021 |archive-date=14 November 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221114072237/https://worldathletics.org/athletes/kenya/eliud-kipchoge-14208194 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.globalsportscommunication.nl/athletes/most-successful-athletes/EN11092-Eliud-Kipchoge.aspx|title=Eliud Kipchoge {{!}} Global Sports Communication|website=globalsportscommunication.nl|language=en|access-date=21 September 2018|archive-date=21 September 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180921225844/https://www.globalsportscommunication.nl/athletes/most-successful-athletes/EN11092-Eliud-Kipchoge.aspx|url-status=dead}}</ref> He ran {{convert|3|km|0|spell=in}} to school on a daily basis.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.runnersworld.com
ews/a20793538/the-simple-life-of-one-of-the-worlds-best-marathoners/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Kipchoge was raised by a single mother (a teacher), and only knew his father from pictures. He is the youngest of four children. He met his trainer [[Patrick Sang]] (a former Olympic medalist in the [[Steeplechase (athletics)|steeplechase]]) in 2001 at the age of 16.<ref name="nytimes201809">{{cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/14/sports/eliud-kipchoge-marathon.html |title=Eliud Kipchoge Is the Greatest Marathoner, Ever |last=Cacciola |first=Scott |date=14 September 2018 |newspaper=The New York Times |access-date=28 September 2018 |archive-date=28 September 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180928161236/https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/14/sports/eliud-kipchoge-marathon.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

Kipchoge's wife and three children live in [[Eldoret]], Kenya.<ref name="auto1">{{Cite news |url=https://www.welt.de/sport/article181548434/Berlin-Marathon-2018-Kenianer-Eliud-Kipchoge-knackt-den-Weltrekord.html |title=Berlin-Marathon 2018: Kenianer Eliud Kipchoge knackt den Weltrekord |last=WELT |date=16 September 2018 |work=DIE WELT |access-date=29 September 2018 |archive-date=19 September 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180919100330/https://www.welt.de/sport/article181548434/Berlin-Marathon-2018-Kenianer-Eliud-Kipchoge-knackt-den-Weltrekord.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/16/sports/eliud-kipchoge-marathon-record.html |title=Berlin Marathon Results: Eliud Kipchoge Breaks World Record |newspaper=The New York Times |date=16 September 2018 |access-date=1 October 2018 |language=en |last1=Cacciola |first1=Scott |last2=Zaveri |first2=Mihir |archive-date=1 October 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181001105559/https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/16/sports/eliud-kipchoge-marathon-record.html |url-status=live }}</ref> He lives and trains in [[Kaptagat]], 30&nbsp;km (19 miles) from Eldoret.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.iaaf.org
ews/feature/eliud-kipchoge-rio-olympic-games|url-status=live}}</ref> He is a devout [[Catholic Church|Catholic]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lerner |first=Ryan |title=Running on Faith: Excited for What's Ahead |url=https://stm.yale.edu/blog/running-on-faith-excited-for-whats-ahead |access-date=14 July 2023 |website=stm.yale.edu |language=en-us}}</ref>

Following the death of [[Kelvin Kiptum]] in 2024, Kipchoge and his family faced online threats falsely accusing him of being involved in Kiptum's death. These threats impacted his training and well-being.<ref>{{Cite web |date=7 May 2024 |title=Kipchoge talks online abuse after Kiptum death |url=https://www.espn.com/olympics/story/_/id/40099361/kipchoge-reveals-impact-abuse-kiptum-death |access-date=21 April 2025 |website=ESPN.com |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=7 May 2024 |title=Eliud Kipchoge's family threatened after death of Kelvin Kiptum |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/athletics/articles/cd13ed985g1o |access-date=21 April 2025 |website=BBC Sport |language=en-GB}}</ref>

==Career==
===2002–2004===
In 2002, he won at the Kenyan trials for the [[2002 IAAF World Cross Country Championships]] junior race. At the World Cross Country Championships, held in [[Dublin]], Kipchoge finished fifth in the individual race and was part of the Kenyan junior team that won gold. Kipchoge also won the 5000 metres race at the Kenyan trial for the [[2002 World Junior Championships in Athletics]] but fell ill and missed the championships. He won the junior race at the [[2003 IAAF World Cross Country Championships]].

He set a [[List of world junior records in athletics|world junior record]] in the 5000&nbsp;m at the 2003 [[Bislett Games]], running a time of 12:52.61 minutes. This stood as the world and [[List of African junior records in athletics|African junior record]] until 2012 when it was improved to 12:47.53 minutes by [[Hagos Gebrhiwet]] of Ethiopia.<ref>[https://www.iaaf.org/records/all-time-toplists/middlelong/5000-metres/outdoor/men/u20?regionType=world&page=1&bestResultsOnly=true&firstDay=1900-01-01&lastDay=2018-09-22 u20 outdoor 5000 Metres men] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180922173810/https://www.iaaf.org/records/all-time-toplists/middlelong/5000-metres/outdoor/men/u20?regionType=world&page=1&bestResultsOnly=true&firstDay=1900-01-01&lastDay=2018-09-22 |date=22 September 2018 }}. IAAF. Retrieved 22 September 2018.</ref>

Kipchoge won a gold medal at the [[2003 World Championships in Athletics – Men's 5000 metres|5000 m]] final at the [[2003 World Championships in Athletics|2003 World Championships in Paris]], outsprinting runner-up [[Hicham El Guerrouj]], the world record holder in the [[1500 metres]] and [[mile run|mile]], by four-hundredths of a second in 12:52.79.<ref>2003 World Championships, {{cite news |url=http://www.iaaf.org
ews/unheralded-kipchoge-salvages-kenyan-pride |url-status=live }}</ref>

In July, he participated in the Golden League 2004 Roma Meeting. In the 5000&nbsp;m event, he dipped first among the starters with 12:46.53, which made him the sixth-fastest ever in the event.<ref>[https://www.iaaf.org/records/all-time-toplists/middlelong/5000-metres/outdoor/men/senior?regionType=world&page=1&bestResultsOnly=true&firstDay=1900-01-01&lastDay=2018-09-16 5000 Metres All Time] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180916233219/https://www.iaaf.org/records/all-time-toplists/middlelong/5000-metres/outdoor/men/senior?regionType=world&page=1&bestResultsOnly=true&firstDay=1900-01-01&lastDay=2018-09-16 |date=16 September 2018 }} (4 October 2010). Retrieved 15 October 2010.</ref>

In 2004, Kipchoge won a bronze medal at the [[Athletics at the 2004 Summer Olympics – Men's 5000 metres|5000 m final]] at the [[2004 Summer Olympics|2004 Athens Olympics]], behind El Guerrouj and [[Kenenisa Bekele]].<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.rediff.com/sports/2004/aug/29oly-ath.htm |title=El Guerrouj completes historic double |date=29 August 2004 |access-date=27 April 2016 |work=[[Rediff.com]] |archive-date=18 October 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161018101439/http://www.rediff.com/sports/2004/aug/29oly-ath.htm |url-status=live }}</ref> He also won the [[Trofeo Alasport]] cross country race earlier that season.

=== 2006–2009 ===
Kipchoge won the bronze in the 3000 metres indoor at the [[2006 IAAF World Indoor Championships|2006 World Championships]] in Moscow.

At the end of the year, Kipchoge won the [[San Silvestre Vallecana]] New Year's Eve 10&nbsp;km road race in a time of 26:54 minutes, which beat his own course record by 40 seconds. This time was also better than the 10K road world record at the time but was run on a downhill course.<ref>{{cite news |date=1 January 2007 |url=https://www.worldathletics.org
ews/kipchoge-breaks-27-minute-barrier-in-madrid- |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[File:Osaka07 D6A M5000M Heat2-2.jpg|thumb|left|Kipchoge (third from the right) during the 5000&nbsp;m heat at the [[2007 World Championships in Athletics|2007 World Championships]] in [[Osaka]]. He won a silver medal in the final.]]Kipchoge won a silver medal at the 5000&nbsp;m final of the [[2007 World Championships in Athletics|2007 World Championships]] at [[Osaka]] in 13:46.00, behind [[Bernard Lagat]] (13:45.87).<ref>2007 World Championships,
 {{cite web |url=http://osaka2007.iaaf.org/results/gender=M/discipline=5000/combCode=hash/roundCode=f/result.html |title=5000&nbsp;m results |publisher=[[International Association of Athletics Federations|IAAF]] |access-date=3 September 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071026134911/http://osaka2007.iaaf.org/results/gender%3DM/discipline%3D5000/combCode%3Dhash/roundCode%3Df/result.html |archive-date=26 October 2007 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

During the [[Athletics at the 2008 Summer Olympics|2008 Olympics]] held in Beijing, China, Kipchoge won a silver medal in the 5000&nbsp;m event with a time of 13:02.80; although better than the previous Olympic record of 13:05.59, it was not enough to match Kenenisa Bekele's pace, who won the gold medal for this race.<ref>2008 Olympics, {{cite magazine |url=http://2008olympics.runnersworld.com/2008/08/mens-5000m-fina.html |title=5000m results |magazine=[[Runner's World]] |access-date=24 August 2008 |archive-date=25 August 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080825022924/http://2008olympics.runnersworld.com/2008/08/mens-5000m-fina.html |url-status=dead}}</ref> On the circuit, he won the [[Great Yorkshire Run]] 10K and [[Campaccio]] Cross Country that year.

He failed to reach the podium at the [[2009 World Championships in Athletics]], finishing in fifth place. He also finished ninth in the 3000&nbsp;m at the [[2009 IAAF World Athletics Final]].

===2010–2011===
He made his debut on the [[2010 IAAF Diamond League]] by winning the 5000&nbsp;m [[Qatar Athletic Super Grand Prix]] in a meet record time.<ref>{{cite news |last=Ramsak |first=Bob |date=14 May 2010 |publisher=IAAF |access-date=27 April 2016 |url=http://www.iaaf.org
ews/report/rudisha-and-powell-impress-as-iaaf-diamond-le |url-status=live }}</ref>

Kipchoge then entered the Carlsbad 5000 in California, United States. The Carlsbad 5&nbsp;km road race is the venue for the world's best times for a 5k road race for men and women, respectively. The fastest to cover the track was Sammy Kipketer in 2000, with 12:59.52 min.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://arrs.run/working_groups_road_records_english.php |title=ARRS – Association of Road Racing Statisticians |website=arrs.run |language=en |access-date=21 September 2018 |archive-date=21 September 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180921191231/https://arrs.run/working_groups_road_records_english.php |url-status=live }}</ref> Kipchoge made a world best attempt, and although he won the race, weather affected his chances, and he finished in 13:11, the fourth-fastest ever for the course up to that point in time.<ref>{{cite news |last=Cruz |first=Dan |date=12 April 2010 |url=http://www.iaaf.org
ews/menjo-takes-five-seconds-off-course-record-in |title=Menjo takes five seconds off course record in Belgrade |publisher=IAAF |access-date=27 April 2016 |archive-date=27 April 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160427133120/http://www.iaaf.org
ews/menjo-takes-five-seconds-off-course-record-in |url-status=live }}</ref>

At the start of 2011, he won the short race at the [[Great Edinburgh Cross Country]], ahead of [[Asbel Kiprop]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Wenig |first=Jörg |date=8 January 2011 |publisher=IAAF |access-date=27 April 2016 |url=http://www.iaaf.org
ews/chebii-clocks-5905-course-record-in-lille-hal |title=Chebii clocks 59:05 course record in Lille Half Marathon |last1=Ramsak |first1=Bob |date=2 September 2012 |access-date=19 January 2013 |publisher=IAAF |last2=Juck |first2=Alfons |archive-date=11 February 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130211195412/http://www.iaaf.org
ews/chebii-clocks-5905-course-record-in-lille-hal |url-status=live }}</ref> The run was won by a new course record time of 59:05 (previously 59:36 by ilahun Regassa set in 2008) by Ezekiel Chebii (former personal_best 59:22), trailed by Bernard Koech 59:10, and Kipchoge earned a third place with 59:25. His time of 59:25 became the second fastest Half Marathon debut, only second to Moses Mosop's 59:20 in Milan in 2010.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.iaaf.org
ews/chebii-clocks-5905-course-record-in-lille-hal|title=IAAF: Chebii clocks 59:05 course record in Lille Half Marathon{{!}} News {{!}} iaaf.org|website=iaaf.org|access-date=28 September 2018|archive-date=17 September 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180917225227/https://www.iaaf.org
ews/chebii-clocks-5905-course-record-in-lille-hal|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 6 October 2012, Kipchoge ran at the [[2012 IAAF World Half Marathon Championships]] in [[Kavarna]], Bulgaria. [[Zersenay Tadese]] of Eritrea won in 1:00:19 and Kipchoge placed sixth in 1:01:52.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.iaaf.org/results/iaaf-world-half-marathon-championships/2012/iaaf-world-half-marathon-championships-4862/men/half-marathon/final/result|title=Half Marathon Result {{!}} IAAF World Half Marathon Championships {{!}} iaaf.org|website=iaaf.org|access-date=1 April 2019|archive-date=1 April 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190401215209/https://www.iaaf.org/results/iaaf-world-half-marathon-championships/2012/iaaf-world-half-marathon-championships-4862/men/half-marathon/final/result|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:Wilson Kipsang Kiprotich running world record at Berlin marathon 2013.jpg|thumb|left|Wilson Kipsang (front) and Kipchoge (behind) running in the [[2013 Berlin Marathon]] in which Kipsang set the world record with 2:03:23 and Kipchoge, racing in his second marathon, finished second, 42 seconds later.]]

===2013===
Kipchoge opened his 2013 season with a win at the [[Barcelona Half Marathon]] in a time of one hour and four seconds.<ref>[http://mitjabarcelona.com/resultados/2013/ Results] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180920084416/http://mitjabarcelona.com/resultados/2013/ |date=20 September 2018 }}. MitjaBarcelona. Retrieved 22 April 2013.</ref> Making his [[marathon]] debut in April, he demonstrated a smooth transition to the longer distance by taking the [[Hamburg Marathon]] title with a run of 2:05:30 hours (with a half marathon split time of 1:03:13 hours), beating the field by over two minutes and setting a new course record.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Haspa Marathon Hamburg 2013: Eliud Kipchoge {{!}} Result |url=https://hamburg.r.mikatiming.de/2013/?content=detail&event=HML&idp=000017074FFEDA000021B430&pid=list&search%5Bsex%5D=M |access-date=2026-03-25 |website=Mika timing |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Minshull |first=Phil |date=21 April 2013 |url=http://www.iaaf.org
ews/report/kipchoge-makes-marvellous-marathon-debut-with |title=Kipchoge makes marvellous Marathon debut with 2:05:30 course record in Hamburg |publisher=IAAF |access-date=22 April 2013 |archive-date=30 April 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130430141313/http://www.iaaf.org
ews/report/kipchoge-makes-marvellous-marathon-debut-with |url-status=live }}</ref> In August 2013, he won the Half Marathon of [[Klagenfurt]] in 1:01:02 minutes.<ref>[http://results.kleinezeitung.at/results/show_results_db.php?veranstnr=12284&racenr=1 Klagenfurt – Kärnten läuft – Halbmarathon – 2013-08-18] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130826054806/http://results.kleinezeitung.at/results/show_results_db.php?veranstnr=12284&racenr=1 |date=26 August 2013 }} Retrieved 19 August 2013.</ref>

Then, he raced in the [[2013 Berlin Marathon]] and finished second in 2:04:05 (with a half marathon split time of 1:01:32 hours), the fifth-fastest time in history, in his second-ever marathon,<ref>{{Cite web |title=Urkunde {{!}} 40. BMW Berlin Marathon |trans-title=Results {{!}} 40. BMW Berlin Marathon |language=de |url=https://api.results.scc-events.com/cert/755?ei=BM&t=BM_2013&l=en&y=2013}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.iaaf.org
ews/report/wilson-kipsang-sets-world-record-of-20323-in|title=IAAF: Kipsang sets world record of 2:03:23 at Berlin Marathon|website=iaaf.org|access-date=29 April 2018|archive-date=26 March 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180326142253/https://www.iaaf.org
ews/report/wilson-kipsang-sets-world-record-of-20323-in|url-status=live}}</ref> behind [[Wilson Kipsang Kiprotich|Wilson Kipsang]], who set a new marathon world record with 2:03:23. Third place went to [[Geoffrey Kamworor]] of Kenya with 2:06:26.<ref name="BBC Sport" /> This was the ninth world record set at the Berlin Marathon.<ref name="BerlinRecords">{{Cite web |url=https://www.runnersworld.de
ews/2015/02/2015-rak-half-marathon-recap-marvelous-mary-keitany-remember-drone/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Kipchoge ran 2:04:42 to win the [[2015 London Marathon]] in April. He also won the [[2015 Berlin Marathon]] later in the year. His win and then-personal best time (2:04:00) occurred even though his shoes malfunctioned, causing his insoles to flap out of both shoes from 10&nbsp;km onward; rather than risk time lost from an adjustment, he finished the race with bloodied, blistered feet.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://www.runnersworld.com
ews/a20853873/kipchoge-wins-berlin-marathon-despite-shoe-malfunction/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

===2016===
In April, Kipchoge won the [[2016 London Marathon]] for the second consecutive year in a time of 2:03:05.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://bleacherreport.com/articles/2635264-london-marathon-2016-results-men-and-womens-top-finishers |title=Retrieved 22 May 2016. |website=[[Bleacher Report]] |access-date=22 May 2016 |archive-date=5 June 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160605011107/http://bleacherreport.com/articles/2635264-london-marathon-2016-results-men-and-womens-top-finishers |url-status=live }}</ref> His performance broke the course record in London and became the second-fastest marathon time in history, missing [[Dennis Kimetto]]'s world record by 8 seconds.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.iaaf.org/records/all-time-toplists/road-running/marathon/outdoor/men/senior?regionType=world&drop=regular&fiftyPercentRule=regular&page=1&bestResultsOnly=true&firstDay=1899-12-31&lastDay=2016-04-24 |title=Retrieved 20 September 2018. |access-date=20 September 2018 |archive-date=20 September 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180920122639/https://www.iaaf.org/records/all-time-toplists/road-running/marathon/outdoor/men/senior?regionType=world&drop=regular&fiftyPercentRule=regular&page=1&bestResultsOnly=true&firstDay=1899-12-31&lastDay=2016-04-24 |url-status=live }}</ref>

'''Rio Olympic Games'''

As the prerace favourite, during the [[Athletics at the 2016 Summer Olympics|2016 Rio Summer Olympics]], Kipchoge gained a gold medal in the [[athletics at the 2016 Summer Olympics|marathon]] event.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.runnersworld.com
ews/a20818616/mens-marathon-eliud-kipchoge-wins-galen-rupp-gets-bronze/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/olympics/36727865 |title=Rio Olympics 2016: Kenya's Eliud Kipchoge wins men's marathon |date=21 August 2016 |work=BBC Sport |access-date=26 September 2018 |language=en-GB |archive-date=26 September 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180926212641/https://www.bbc.com/sport/olympics/36727865 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.latimes.com/sports/olympics/la-sp-oly-rio-2016-kipchoge-of-kenya-wins-men-s-marathon-1471791537-htmlstory.html |title=Rio Olympics: Eliud Kipchoge of Kenya wins men's marathon; Galen Rupp of the U.S. takes bronze |last=Dillman |first=Lisa |website=[[Los Angeles Times]] |date=27 August 2016 |access-date=26 September 2018 |archive-date=26 September 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180926205804/http://www.latimes.com/sports/olympics/la-sp-oly-rio-2016-kipchoge-of-kenya-wins-men-s-marathon-1471791537-htmlstory.html |url-status=live }}</ref> On the last day of the Olympic Games on 21 August 2016, he won in a time of 2:08:44. The runner up was Feyisa Lilesa (Ethiopia) in 2:09:54 and the bronze medal went to Galen Rupp (USA), doing his second marathon, crossing the finish line in 2:10:05. When the halfway point after 21.0975&nbsp;km was reached, 37 men were within 10 seconds of the lead runner. The participants' field diminished to 3 lead runners shortly before 34&nbsp;km. Kipchoge made his final move on silver medal winner Lilesa around 36&nbsp;km into the race. He covered the first half of the race in 1:05:55 while doing the second half in 1:02:49, which amounts to a difference of more than 3 minutes, a negative split.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.iaaf.org
ews/report/rio-2016-men-marathon|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Ingle">{{Cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2016/aug/21/eliud-kipchoge-callum-hawkins-feyisa-lilesa-olympic-marathon |title=Eliud Kipchoge powers to marathon gold as Callum Hawkins finishes ninth |last=Ingle |first=Sean |date=21 August 2016 |website=The Guardian |language=en |access-date=28 September 2018 |archive-date=21 August 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160821222328/https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2016/aug/21/eliud-kipchoge-callum-hawkins-feyisa-lilesa-olympic-marathon |url-status=live }}</ref> The winning gap between Kipchoge and Lilesa by 70 seconds was the largest victory margin since the [[Athletics at the 1972 Summer Olympics – Men's marathon|1972 Olympic marathon]].<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.flotrack.org/articles/5054839-eliud-kipchoge-wins-olympic-marathon-galen-rupp-takes-bronze |title=Eliud Kipchoge Wins Olympic Marathon, Galen Rupp Takes Bronze |access-date=28 September 2018 |language=en-US |archive-date=29 September 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180929000314/https://www.flotrack.org/articles/5054839-eliud-kipchoge-wins-olympic-marathon-galen-rupp-takes-bronze |url-status=live }}</ref> Kipchoge's winning time of 2:08:44 was, as of August 2021, his slowest marathon time. One hundred fifty-five runners started the race, the largest field in Olympic history; 140 of them finished the race.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.iaaf.org/competitions/olympic-games/the-xxxi-olympic-games-5771/results/men/marathon/final/result|title=IAAF: Marathon Result {{!}} The XXXI Olympic Games {{!}} iaaf.org|website=iaaf.org|access-date=29 September 2018|archive-date=29 September 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180929233451/https://www.iaaf.org/competitions/olympic-games/the-xxxi-olympic-games-5771/results/men/marathon/final/result|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=http://www.espn.com/espnw/sports/article/17359189/eliud-kipchoge-kenya-wins-olympic-men-marathon-american-galen-rupp-takes-bronze |title=Eliud Kipchoge of Kenya wins Olympic men's marathon; American Galen Rupp takes bronze |work=espnW |access-date=28 September 2018 |archive-date=29 September 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180929000531/http://www.espn.com/espnw/sports/article/17359189/eliud-kipchoge-kenya-wins-olympic-men-marathon-american-galen-rupp-takes-bronze |url-status=live }}</ref> With this win, Kipchoge became the second Kenyan male after Sammy Wanjiru in [[Athletics at the 2008 Summer Olympics|Beijing 2008]] to win an Olympic marathon gold medal. At the same Olympics, the women's marathon was won by [[Jemima Sumgong]], who became the first female Kenyan winner.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2016/aug/14/jemima-sumgong-wins-olympic-marathon-gold-kenya |title=Jemima Sumgong wins Olympic marathon gold for Kenya |author=<!--not stated--> |agency=Reuters |date=14 August 2016 |website=The Guardian |language=en |access-date=1 October 2018 |archive-date=2 October 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181002020158/https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2016/aug/14/jemima-sumgong-wins-olympic-marathon-gold-kenya |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="Ingle" />

On 20 November 2016, Kipchoge ran in the Airtel [[Delhi Half Marathon]], winning the race, clocking a time of 59:44.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://en.omriyadat.com/african-athletics/kenya-eliud-kipchoge-marathon |title=Rio 2016: Kenya's Kipchoge triumphs in men's marathon |publisher=OmRiyadat English |access-date=21 August 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161018073703/http://en.omriyadat.com/african-athletics/kenya-eliud-kipchoge-marathon |archive-date=18 October 2016 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

===2017===
{{Further|Breaking2}}

On 6 May, Kipchoge, along with Zersenay Tadese (then world record holder in the half marathon) and [[Lelisa Desisa]] (2-time Boston Marathon winner), attempted the first sub-two-hour assisted marathon in the Nike [[Breaking2]] project on the [[Monza]] [[Autodromo Nazionale Monza|Formula 1 racetrack]] near [[Milan]], Italy. All three runners ran a test 2 months before the attempt. The target time was 1 hour for a half Marathon. Kipchoge finished first in 59:17. The course was measured at 2400&nbsp;m per lap.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.runnersworld.com
ews/a20849569/sub-2-marathon-test-run-yields-fast-times-lingering-questions/ |url-status=live }}</ref> During the 2-hour attempt, the runners were paced by a lead car and 30 supporting pacers joining in stages (both considered illegal under [[IAAF]] rules).<ref name="veconomist">{{cite news |author=J.S. |title=Can the marathon's two-hour barrier be broken? |url=https://www.economist.com/blogs/gametheory/2017/10/runnin-down-dream |newspaper=[[The Economist]] |date=4 October 2017 |access-date=4 October 2017 |archive-date=15 May 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180515095632/https://www.economist.com/blogs/gametheory/2017/10/runnin-down-dream |url-status=live }}</ref> The race started at 5:45h local time on the 2.4&nbsp;km track. Kipchoge finished in 2:00:25, while the other two had to slow and finished far behind.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.herbertsteffny.de/artikel/Nike_Sub_2Hours.htm |title=Sub 2:00 Hours Marathon – kritisch hinterfragt |website=herbertsteffny.de |language=de-de |access-date=29 September 2018 |archive-date=13 October 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181013041808/http://www.herbertsteffny.de/artikel/Nike_Sub_2Hours.htm |url-status=live }}</ref> The runners planned even 14:13 5k splits to break 2 hours. His 5k splits were: 14:14, 14:07, 14:13, 14:15, 14:14, 14:17, 14:17, 14:27, and 6:20 to finish.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/live/2017/may/06/sub-two-hour-marathon-attempt-in-monza-live |title=Eliud Kipchoge misses sub two-hour marathon target in Monza – as it happened |last=Caroll |first=James |date=6 May 2017 |work=The Guardian |access-date=30 April 2018 |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077 |archive-date=22 April 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180422224117/https://www.theguardian.com/sport/live/2017/may/06/sub-two-hour-marathon-attempt-in-monza-live |url-status=live }}</ref> The 5k split times from 25k and further would be world records: 25k in 1:11:03, 30k in 1:25:20, 35k in 1:39:37, 40k in 1:54:04.

On 24 September, he won the [[2017 Berlin Marathon]] in a time of 2:03:32.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.bmw-berlin-marathon.com/en
ews/2017/09/24/eliud-kipchoge-wins-dramatic-race-in-berlin-with-world-leading-time.html |url-status=live }}</ref> In rainy conditions, he finished 14 seconds ahead of [[Guye Adola]] who ran his first marathon, and set the fastest marathon debut ever.<ref>{{Cite news |url=http://www.bz-berlin.de/berlin/44-berlin-marathon-eliud-kipchoge-ergebnis-zeit-weltrekord |title=44. Berlin-Marathon: Eliud Kipchoge siegt, Weltrekord verpasst |access-date=30 April 2018 |archive-date=18 September 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180918193912/https://www.bz-berlin.de/berlin/44-berlin-marathon-eliud-kipchoge-ergebnis-zeit-weltrekord |url-status=live }}</ref> Former marathon world record holder Wilson Kipsang and 2016 winner Kenenisa Bekele failed to finish.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.welt.de/sport/article168971522/Kipchoge-schrammt-am-Weltrekord-vorbei.html |title=Berlin-Marathon: Eliod Kipchoge schrammt am Weltrekord vorbei |last=SUF |date=24 September 2017 |work=DIE WELT |access-date=30 April 2018 |archive-date=22 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210422172118/https://www.welt.de/sport/article168971522/Kipchoge-schrammt-am-Weltrekord-vorbei.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.rbb24.de/sport/thema/2017/berlin-marathon-2017/beitraege/44-berlin-marathon-strecke-lauf-sonntag.html |title=Favorit Kipchoge gewinnt den 44. Berlin-Marathon |website=rbb24.de |language=de |access-date=30 April 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180704071652/https://www.rbb24.de/sport/thema/2017/berlin-marathon-2017/beitraege/44-berlin-marathon-strecke-lauf-sonntag.html |archive-date=4 July 2018 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

===2018===
Kipchoge won the [[2018 London Marathon|London Marathon]] in a time of 2:04:17 with a half marathon split time of 1:01:00. In a time of 2:04:49  Shura Kitata (Ethiopia) came in second and  [[Mo Farah]] (Great Britain) finished in 2:06:21, a British record. Kenenisa Bekele finished sixth 2:08:53 and defending champion [[Daniel Wanjiru]].<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.eurosport.de/leichtathletik/olympiasieger-kipchoge-siegt-beim-london-marathon-farah-mit-britischem-rekord_sto6723468/story.shtml |title=London-Marathon mit Hattrick durch Kipchoge und Rekord für Farah |date=22 April 2018 |work=Eurosport Deutschland |language=de |access-date=22 April 2018 |archive-date=22 April 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180422202245/https://www.eurosport.de/leichtathletik/olympiasieger-kipchoge-siegt-beim-london-marathon-farah-mit-britischem-rekord_sto6723468/story.shtml |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/athletics/43855468 |title=London Marathon 2017: Mo Farah finishes third as Eliud Kipchoge wins |date=22 April 2018 |work=BBC Sport |access-date=22 April 2018 |language=en-GB |archive-date=22 April 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180422115823/https://www.bbc.com/sport/athletics/43855468 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.nzz.ch/sport/kenenisa-bekele-der-koenig-im-sauseschritt-ld.1387169|title=Kenenisa Bekele: Der König im Sauseschritt {{!}} NZZ|last=Geisser|first=Remo|date=19 May 2018|work=Neue Zürcher Zeitung|access-date=17 September 2018|language=de-CH|issn=0376-6829|archive-date=17 September 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180917143508/https://www.nzz.ch/sport/kenenisa-bekele-der-koenig-im-sauseschritt-ld.1387169|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/live/2018/apr/22/london-marathon-2018-live |title=London Marathon 2018: Kipchoge wins men's race with Farah third as Cheruiyot takes women's—as it happened |last1=Harris |first1=Daniel |date=22 April 2018 |work=The Guardian |access-date=30 April 2018 |last2=Unwin |first2=Will |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077 |archive-date=30 April 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180430050250/https://www.theguardian.com/sport/live/2018/apr/22/london-marathon-2018-live |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=London Marathon 2015 men results, Eliud Kipchoge wins |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/othersports/athletics/london-marathon/11559741/london-marathon-2015-results-winners-men.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220112/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/othersports/athletics/london-marathon/11559741/london-marathon-2015-results-winners-men.html |archive-date=12 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |last=Bloom |first=Ben |date=26 April 2015 |access-date=28 April 2015}}{{cbignore}}</ref> eighth 2:10:35.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Kipchoge and Cheruiyot ensure Kenyan double at London Marathon {{!}} REPORTS {{!}} World Athletics |url=https://worldathletics.org/competitions/world-athletics-label-road-races
ews/london-marathon-2018-kipchoge-cheruiyot |access-date=2026-05-16 |website=worldathletics.org}}</ref> Kipchoge confirmed question on attempting to break the Marathon world record. The women's race saw a win by Vivian Cheriuyot (Kenya) with a time of 2:18:31 the 4th best time ever and Brigid Kosgei (Kenya) place second in 2:20:13.

